<description>
Crop out the unique region marked by a complete monochrome rectangular frame.

Input structure
The input is a larger patterned grid containing exactly one color whose pixels
form the full perimeter of a rectangle and appear nowhere else.

Rule
Find that complete rectangular frame and return the input cells inside its
bounding box, including the frame itself.

Key insight
The wanted output is not rebuilt from the repeated background pattern; it is
the exact subgrid selected by the only color that forms a complete frame.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "larger patterned grid containing a unique complete monochrome rectangular frame",
  "transform": "crop to the frame bbox, preserving the frame border and all interior pixels unchanged",
  "grid_shape": "same height and width as the selected frame bbox"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid:
    shape: NxN square, N in 24-28
  background:
    structure: outer-product-max pattern from 1D basis vector v of length N
    rule: pixel(r,c) = max(v[r], v[c]) under a custom total ordering of values
    basis_vector_v:
      separator: one color appearing at regular intervals (spacing 3 or 6) forming full-grid horizontal and vertical lines
      block_size: number of positions between separators, 2-5
      blocks: between separators, each block of k positions contains values from {0} union content_colors
      first_two_positions: always 0,0 [observed]
    colors:
      bg: 0 (minimum in ordering)
      content: 2-3 distinct non-zero colors
      separator: 1 color acting as grid lines (full rows and cols of this color)
      total_ordering: 0 < content/sep values under a custom total order; f(a,b)=max(a,b) under this order
    symmetry: rv == cv (same vector for rows and columns)
  frame:
    color: unique color not used in background, appears ONLY as a complete rectangular perimeter
    shape: square, 6-7 cells per side
    placement: anywhere on the grid; may or may not align with separator positions
    exactly_one: solver asserts exactly 1 color forms a complete monochrome rectangular frame
  interior: frame interior preserves the background pattern unchanged
  output: crop of input at the frame bounding box (including the frame border)

generation_strategy:
  approach: background-first, then overlay frame
  steps:
    1. Choose N in [24,28], block_size k in {2,5}, content_count in {2,3}
    2. Pick distinct colors for content (c1..cn), separator (sep), frame (fc), plus 0 as background
    3. Build separator positions at k, 2k+1, 3k+2, ... up to N-1
    4. Define a total ordering on {0, c1..cn} and assign v-values to non-separator positions in blocks
    5. Set v[sep_pos] = sep for all separator positions; rv = cv = v
    6. Compute full NxN grid as pixel(r,c) = max(v[r], v[c]) under the chosen ordering
    7. Choose frame size S in {6,7} and valid top-left (r0,c0) so frame fits within grid
    8. Verify fc does not collide with any background color; draw frame perimeter with fc
    9. Verify no other color in the grid accidentally forms a complete rectangular frame (>=3x3)
    10. Output = grid[r0:r0+S, c0:c0+S]
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

